Now write a minimal test to validate infra, then expand. Actually let's write the full suite in one go, then iterate on failures.

Design tests:

```java
package io.undertow.io;

imports...

@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class) maybe not needed; use Mockito.mock directly.

class BlockingReceiverImplReceiveFullBytesTests {
    private ServerConnection connection;
    private ByteBufferPool pool;
    private HttpServerExchange exchange;
    private HeaderMap requestHeaders;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        requestHeaders = new HeaderMap();
        connection = mock(ServerConnection.class);
        pool = mock(ByteBufferPool.class);
        when(connection.getByteBufferPool()).thenReturn(pool);
        when(pool.getArrayBackedPool()).thenReturn(pool);
        exchange = mock(HttpServerExchange.class);
        when(exchange.getRequestHeaders()).thenReturn(requestHeaders);
        when(exchange.getConnection()).thenReturn(connection);
        when(exchange.isRequestComplete()).thenReturn(false);
        when(exchange.isResponseStarted()).thenReturn(false);
        when(exchange.endExchange()).thenReturn(exchange);
    }

    private PooledByteBuffer poolReturning(int size) {
        PooledByteBuffer pooled = mock(PooledByteBuffer.class);
        when(pooled.getBuffer()).thenReturn(ByteBuffer.allocate(size));
        when(pool.allocate()).thenReturn(pooled);
        return pooled;
    }
```

Note: Mockito strict stubs might complain about unnecessary stubbing (exchange.isResponseStarted stub unused unless error callback null). Using `mock()` directly without MockitoExtension → no strictness issues. Good; avoid @ExtendWith.

Tests:

1. `handleReceivesEmptyArrayWhenRequestComplete`:
```java
when(exchange.isRequestComplete()).thenReturn(true);
AtomicReference<byte[]> received; 
receiver.receiveFullBytes((ex, data) -> { received.set(data); });
assertArrayEquals(new byte[0], received.get());
verifyNoInteractions(pool)? Actually pool isn't touched: verify(pool, never()).allocate();
assertSame(exchange, capturedExchange)
```
Also verify no error callback needed.

2. `nullCallbackThrowsIllegalArgumentException`:
```java
IllegalArgumentException ex = assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -> receiver.receiveFullBytes(null, errorCallback));
assertTrue(ex.getMessage().contains("callback"));
```
Message format from jboss logging: "UT000010: Argument cannot be null: %s"? check actual text. Let's grep UndertowMessages id for argumentCannotBeNull. Message annotation for argumentCannotBeNull: probably @Message(id = 10, "Argument cannot be null: %s")? Check to assert message content appropriately. We'll just assert exception type and maybe message contains "callback" (safe). And verify error callback not invoked.

Also test null callback with 2-arg? `receiveFullBytes(null)` (single arg) presumably also throws IllegalArgumentException because it delegates. Include that.

3. `bodyIsDeliveredAndBufferIsClosed`:
```java
byte[] body = "hello world".getBytes(UTF_8);
requestHeaders.put(Headers.CONTENT_LENGTH, body.length);
PooledByteBuffer pooled = poolReturning(8);
receiver with new ByteArrayInputStream(body)
AtomicReference<byte[]> data; 
receiver.receiveFullBytes(callback, error)
assertArrayEquals(body, data.get())
verify(pooled).close()
verifyNoInteractions(errorCb)
```
Also verify callback exchange is same.

4. `chunkedReadsAcrossMultipleBuffers` — body longer than buffer size (e.g., 100 bytes with buffer size 8) using real DefaultByteBufferPool instead of mock? Using mock pool with ByteBuffer.allocate(8) gives multiple reads and loop rewrites same buffer. Use body 100 bytes → verify full body. Good, also verifies loop.

5. `xContentLengthWithoutContentLengthGetsContentLengthHeader`:
```java
requestHeaders.put(Headers.X_CONTENT_LENGTH, body.length);
poolReturning(body.length)
receive
assertArrayEquals
assertEquals(String.valueOf(body.length), requestHeaders.getFirst(Headers.CONTENT_LENGTH));
```

6. `contentLengthGreaterThanMaxIntTriggersRequestToLarge`:
```java
requestHeaders.put(Headers.CONTENT_LENGTH, ((long)Integer.MAX_VALUE)+1);
receiver.receiveFullBytes(callback, errorCb)
ArgumentCaptor<IOException> captor; assertTrue instanceof Receiver.RequestToLargeException; verify callback never invoked; verify(pool, never()).allocate();
```

7. `contentLengthEqualToIntegerMaxValueIsAllowed`? That would allocate ByteArrayOutputStream(Integer.MAX_VALUE) → OOM. No. Skip. Boundary: contentLength == maxBufferSize allowed (not error) but would try reading; with small body and mocked buffer it's fine, verify callback receives data. Good boundary test: maxBufferSize = body.length; no error.

8. `maxBufferSizeExceededTriggersRequestToLarge`: contentLength = 11, maxBufferSize = 10 → error RequestToLargeException, callback not invoked.

9. `maxBufferSizeIgnoredWhenNoContentLength`: no headers, maxBufferSize=2, body 10 bytes → delivers all (since contentLength -1). This is an equivalence class: unknown length not bounded.

10. `ioExceptionFromStreamInvokesErrorCallback`:
custom InputStream throwing IOException("boom") on read → error callback receives same IOException with message, callback not called; verify pooled closed? try-with-resources closes → verify(pooled).close(). Also verify no exception thrown from receiveFullBytes (caught).

11. `nullErrorCallbackOnTooLargeEndsExchange`: no explicit error callback (single-arg receiveFullBytes with content-length > MAX) → exchange.setStatusCode(500) and setPersistent(false) called and endExchange called.
